<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape of Good Hope</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cape of Good Hope is a headland on the southwestern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, rounded by Portuguese navigators in the late fifteenth century. The sea route around it allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> to trade directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>'s monopoly covered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade east of the Cape. The route diverted trade away from the Middle Eastern routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope.jpg" alt="Cape of Good Hope" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Playa_Dias,_Cape_Point,_Sud%C3%A1frica,_2018-07-23,_DD_103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, ended this wasteful competition by merging the rival Voorcompagnieën into the Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie (VOC), granting it a 21-year monopoly of Dutch trade east of the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> and, critically, quasi-sovereign powers to wage war, conclude treaties, build forts and administer justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator its sailors settled Madeira and the Azores, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until Bartolomeu Dias rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive breakthroughs followed: under João II, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> in 1488, demonstrating the viability of an oceanic route to Asia, and under Manuel I, Vasco da Gama completed that route in 1498, reaching Calicut on the Malabar Coast and returning to Lisbon…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Anglo-Dutch Convention of London, the Dutch formally ceded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b>, which Britain had occupied since 1806.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Elizabeth I granted a monopoly of English trade east of the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> to a company of London merchants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasco da Gama left Lisbon with four ships on a voyage that rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> and reached Calicut in 1498.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
